---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import FAQSection from '@components/article/FAQSection.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro';

// Article metadata
const title = 'Essential Travel Tech for Digital Nomads (2025)';
const description = 'The must-have gadgets and accessories for working from anywhere. Compact, reliable tech that fits in a carry-on and keeps you productive on the road.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2025-11-23');
const readingTime = 10;
const image = 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1519389950473-47ba0277781c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80';
const imageAlt = 'Digital nomad travel tech setup with laptop and accessories';

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  {text: 'My Bag Weighs 23 Pounds', slug: 'introduction', depth: 2},
  {text: 'The Non-Negotiables', slug: 'essentials', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Connectivity', slug: 'connectivity', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Productivity Stuff', slug: 'productivity', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Power', slug: 'power', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Organization', slug: 'comfort', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Quick Answers', slug: 'faq', depth: 2},
  {text: 'What I\'ve Learned', slug: 'conclusion', depth: 2},
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Phone as hotspot vs dedicated device?',
    answer: '<p>Phone hotspot works. Dedicated device has better battery and doesn\'t kill your phone. I use my phone but keep it plugged in.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What laptop specs matter?',
    answer: '<p>8GB RAM minimum, 16 preferred. SSD required. 8+ hour battery required. Everything else is personal preference.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Portable monitor worth it?',
    answer: '<p>For staying somewhere months? Maybe. For actual traveling? I don\'t think so. Adds weight and bulk.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How to keep gear safe?',
    answer: '<p>Never check electronics. Never leave stuff unattended. Good travel insurance. Everything backed up to cloud. Common sense.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'International adapters?',
    answer: '<p>One universal adapter that does everything. Make sure it has USB ports. Done.</p>'
  }
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Best Laptops for Remote Work',
    excerpt: 'Find the perfect laptop for productivity on the go.',
    href: '/gear/best-laptops-remote-work',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1496181133206-80ce9b88a853?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Laptop on desk'
  },
  {
    title: 'Top Destinations for Digital Nomads',
    excerpt: 'Cities with great WiFi, affordable living, and thriving communities.',
    href: '/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1528181304800-259b08848526?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Digital nomad destination'
  },
  {
    title: 'How to Find Reliable WiFi Anywhere',
    excerpt: 'Tips and tools for staying connected no matter where you travel.',
    href: '/blog/find-reliable-wifi-anywhere',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1521791136064-7986c2920216?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'WiFi signal concept'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Travel"
    categorySlug="guides/travel"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">My Bag Weighs 23 Pounds. Everything I Need Is In It.</h2>
  <p>
    Three years of nomading. Thirty-seven countries. My packing list has been edited down to exactly what I actually
    use.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've bought so much stuff I ended up throwing away or shipping home. The portable monitor that made sense until I
    carried it through an airport twice. The fancy keyboard that stayed in its case. The backup charger I never actually
    needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's what survived the cuts.
  </p>

  <!-- The Essentials -->
  <h2 id="essentials">The Non-Negotiables</h2>

  <h3>Laptop: MacBook Air M3</h3>
  <p>
    Battery lasts literally all day. Silent because no fan. Light enough to not notice in my bag. Could I save money
    with
    Windows? Sure. But this thing just works without me ever thinking about it.
  </p>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/gear/best-laptops-remote-work">full laptop guide</a> if you want options.
  </p>

  <h3>Headphones: Sony WH-1000XM5</h3>
  <p>
    Cafes are loud. Airports are loud. That hostel common room at 2am is very loud. These turn any space into quiet
    workspace. I've also got AirPods Pro for when I don't want to carry the big ones. Most nomads end up with both
    eventually.
  </p>

  <h3>Mouse: Logitech MX Anywhere 3</h3>
  <p>
    Could use the trackpad. Choose not to. This thing works on literally any surface including my jeans. Sounds
    ridiculous until you're on a bumpy bus trying to click something.
  </p>

  <!-- Connectivity -->
  <h2 id="connectivity">Connectivity (The Stuff That Actually Matters)</h2>

  <h3>Airalo eSIM</h3>
  <p>
    Remember hunting for SIM card shops in foreign airports? Don't miss that at all. Now I just buy data before I land.
    Takes two minutes.
  </p>

  <h3>NordVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Cafe WiFi is basically a public bulletin board for hackers. VPN scrambles everything. Also lets me watch my Netflix
    library from home, which sounds dumb but matters when you're homesick.
  </p>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">VPN comparison</a> for full reviews.
  </p>

  <h3>Anker USB-C Hub</h3>
  <p>
    My laptop has two USB-C ports. That's it. The hub gives me USB-A, HDMI, SD card slot when I need them. Probably use
    it twice a week.
  </p>

  <!-- Productivity Boosters -->
  <h2 id="productivity">Productivity Stuff</h2>

  <h3>Roost Laptop Stand</h3>
  <p>
    Folds completely flat. Weighs nothing. Raises my screen to eye level so my neck doesn't hurt. I resisted getting one
    for way too long. Should have bought it immediately.
  </p>

  <h3>Small Ring Light</h3>
  <p>
    Video calls in hotel rooms look terrible because the lighting is always terrible. Clip-on light fixes it. Cost like
    $20.
  </p>

  <h3>No External Keyboard</h3>
  <p>
    Tried it. Hated carrying it. The MacBook keyboard is fine. Your mileage may vary.
  </p>

  <h3>No Portable Monitor</h3>
  <p>
    Owned one. Carried it for three months. Used it maybe four times. Sold it. If you're staying somewhere for months,
    maybe. For actual traveling? Nah.
  </p>

  <!-- Power Solutions -->
  <h2 id="power">Power</h2>

  <h3>Anker 735 Charger (65W)</h3>
  <p>
    Charges my laptop, phone, and headphones from one brick. Replaced three separate chargers. This is one of those "why
    didn't I do this earlier" things.
  </p>

  <h3>Zendure Universal Adapter</h3>
  <p>
    Works in every country. Has USB ports built in. Lost one in Vietnam and immediately bought the same thing again.
  </p>

  <h3>Anker Power Bank (24,000mAh)</h3>
  <p>
    Long flights. Bus rides through rural wherever. Cafes where every outlet is taken. This has saved me more times than
    I can count. Just barely under airline limits.
  </p>

  <!-- Travel Comfort -->
  <h2 id="comfort">Organization</h2>

  <h3>Peak Design Tech Pouch</h3>
  <p>
    Everything has a spot. Cables, adapters, pens, earbuds, tiny screwdriver for glasses. I can find anything in the
    dark. Expensive but worth it.
  </p>

  <h3>Cables I Actually Carry</h3>
  <p>
    Two USB-C cables (one long, one short). One Lightning cable for my AirPods. One USB-A to USB-C for old ports. That's
    it. Anything else I can buy locally if needed.
  </p>

  <h3>Samsung T7 SSD</h3>
  <p>
    Cloud backup is primary. But internet isn't always reliable. This holds a full backup of everything important. Also
    useful for transferring large files to clients.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <h2 id="faq">Quick Answers</h2>
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">What I've Learned</h2>
  <p>
    Start with less than you think you need. You can buy almost anything almost anywhere. The stuff you forget to pack
    is never as important as it seems at 3am the night before you leave.
  </p>
  <p>
    The gear that survives is the gear you actually use. Everything else just weighs you down.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reliability matters more than features. When your laptop dies in a country where Apple stores don't exist, feature
    lists don't help.
  </p>
  <p>
    Travel light. Work heavy. The point is the work and the travel, not the stuff.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planning your first nomad trip? Check out our <a href="/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads">destination guide</a>
    and make sure you have <a href="/blog/best-travel-insurance-remote-workers">proper travel insurance</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
